From ed01fbd4328d439f8657b68efe3da5b9b4d656ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fountain5405 <Fountain5405@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 15:06:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/9] generalized-bulletproofs: free the l and r coefficient
 vectors after evaluation, zeroized

ArithmeticCircuitStatement::prove evaluates the polynomials l(X) and r(X) at
x, then proves the inner product of the results. The coefficient vectors were
shadowed by the evaluations and kept alive until the function returned, through
the inner-product argument. They hold witness values (aL, aO, sL, the vector
commitments' values, aR * y, sR * y), and nothing ever wiped them:
ScalarVector is not ZeroizeOnDrop.

They are now zeroized and dropped right after evaluation.
- Memory: in a 128-input FCMP++ wallet build (monerodato.3's wallet-rpc on a 12-core Ryzen 9 3900X (16 GB RAM, SATA SSD),
  3 rounds), this step alone lowers the peak by 25 MB of 1,288 MB.
  The peak comes earlier, while the vectors are built (the next two commits).
- Hygiene: the secret coefficients no longer stay in freed memory.
- Constant time: the zeroize is a pass over memory whose length is public (the
  row count). No branch on secret data is added.

The proofs are unchanged (prove_output_unchanged).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../src/arithmetic_circuit_proof.rs                  | 12 ++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/crypto/generalized-bulletproofs/src/arithmetic_circuit_proof.rs b/crypto/generalized-bulletproofs/src/arithmetic_circuit_proof.rs
index 1939f6586..3bc7c5f16 100644
--- a/crypto/generalized-bulletproofs/src/arithmetic_circuit_proof.rs
+++ b/crypto/generalized-bulletproofs/src/arithmetic_circuit_proof.rs
@@ -639,8 +639,16 @@ where
       }
       res
     };
-    let l = poly_eval(&l, &x);
-    let r = poly_eval(&r, &x);
+    let l_eval = poly_eval(&l, &x);
+    let r_eval = poly_eval(&r, &x);
+    // The coefficient vectors hold witness values and are no longer needed: wipe and free them now, instead of keeping
+    // them to the end of this function, through the inner-product argument
+    l.zeroize();
+    r.zeroize();
+    drop(l);
+    drop(r);
+    let l = l_eval;
+    let r = r_eval;
 
     let t_caret = l.inner_product(r.0.iter());
 
